### Proposal Details

This is a proposal for adding a small number of new `iter.Seq`/`iter.Seq2`-related utilities to `package iter`.

This covers a subset of functionality previously discussed in some other proposals:
- https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61898
- https://github.com/golang/go/issues/68947

The first of these was an ambitious effort to provide a full suite of iterator combinator functions, which caused [a lot of different feedback](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61898#issuecomment-2112841326), and eventually it was retracted in https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61898#issuecomment-2877009539 because the discussion could not reach consensus. The second was a proposed extension of the first, which seems to have been closed only because the first was retracted.

In _this_ proposal I'm suggesting a much smaller set of utility functions that deal with some situations that I've encountered frequently in new code making use of `iter.Seq` and `iter.Seq2`. I've chosen this subset carefully to focus on factoring out code that I expect lots of folks are writing already, and intentionally _not_ proposing anything that's intended to help new "iterator-combinator-style" design patterns to emerge.

I'm hoping that by focusing on a smaller scope it'll be easier to come to consensus. Of course, we do still need to consider how `package iter` might evolve under future proposals -- this introduces some functions that could become precedent for the design of other functions later -- but I want to be explicit up front that I _don't_ think that Go should amass a large library of "iterator combinator" functions, because Go just isn't that kind of language: anything involving nontrivial logic should typically be written out as normal control flow with `for` and `if` statements.

With all of that context out of the way, the proposal...

---

```go
package iter

// None is a zero-length [Seq].
func None[V any](func(V) bool) {})

// None2 is a zero-length [Seq2]
func None2[K, V any](func(K, V) bool) {}

// Single returns a [Seq] which produces just the one given value.
func Single[V any](value V) Seq[V] {
	return func(yield func(V) bool) {
		yield(value)
	}    
}

// Single2 returns a [Seq2] which produces just the one given pair of values.
func Single2[K, V any](key K, value V) Seq2[K, V] {
	return func(yield func(K, V) bool) {
		yield(key, value)
	}    
}

// Map returns a sequence of the same length as the given sequence,
// applying the given function to each value in turn.
func Map[V1, V2 any](s Seq[V1], f func(V1) V2) Seq[V2] {
    return func(yield func(V2) bool) {
        for v := range s {
            if !yield(f(v)) {
                return
            }
        }
    }
}


// Map2 returns a sequence of the same length as the given sequence,
// applying the given function to each pair in turn.
func Map2[K1, V1, K2, V2 any](s Seq2[K1, V1], f func(K1, V1) (K2, V2)) Seq2[K2, V2] {
    return func(yield func(K2, V2) bool) {
        for k, v := range s {
            if !yield(f(k, v)) {
                return
            }
        }
    }
}
```

I've included example implementations here mainly just to illustrate that these functions are not hiding anything particularly interesting. As usual, the signatures are the main point of the proposal.

That is the entire proposal. What follows is an attempt to justify why I think this is a good set of functions to start with, and also possibly to end with (i.e. I think this could potentially be the _entire set_ of stdlib iterator composition utilities).

## General observations

What all three of these pairs of functions have in common is that they are all dealing with something trivial -- no special conditional control flow, no fan-in/fan-out, etc -- that nonetheless currently requires a significant amount of noisy boilerplate that communicates nothing of value to the reader of a function performing these operations. Factoring them out into the standard library would, I claim, make code for these straightforward situations considerably easier to read and review, by removing the unhelpful boilerplate and making it abundantly clear to a reader that nothing unusual is going on and so they can focus their attention elsewhere.

There is no other reasonable way to implement these particular operations except to inline the bodies of these functions into your own program. (This is an assertion about their implementations rather than the names and signatures I chose; I do acknowledge that some may prefer other names, and that some prefer different signatures for `None`/`None2`/`Map`/`Map2` based on discussion in the other issues.)

All three of these pairs of functions are aimed mainly at situations where one is writing a concrete implementation of an interface whose design is more general than the specific implementation being written, and so the author needs to write a small amount of adapter code to implement the general API in terms of more specific implementation details. When implementing an interface that requires returning a sequence, there is no simpler for-loop-only alternative: the result must be a sequence one way or another. None of these are intended to replace normal conditional branching with `for` and `if` statements when more complex logic is needed, and none are intended for situations where sequences are not already required by the interface being implemented (and so, for example, one could just iterate over a concrete slice or map in a normal loop instead).

It remains annoying that we need both single and pair variants of every general-purpose utility related to sequences. However, I think the arguments made about that in https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61898#issuecomment-2112841326 are reasonable, particularly when limited only to this small set of three pairs of functions.

## `None`/`None2`

Unlike with slices and maps, the zero value of `iter.Seq` and `iter.Seq2` cannot be used to cause zero iterations of a `for ... range` loop. Therefore if a specific concrete implementation of a sequence-returning interface method needs to return a zero-length sequence it needs to return a non-nil pointer to _something_.

`None` and `None2` are intended to be used as that something:

```go
func (e *Example) SeqReturningThing() iter.Seq[string] {
    return iter.None[string]
}

func (e *Example) Seq2ReturningThing() iter.Seq2[string, string] {
    return iter.None2[string, string]
}
```

Note that `iter.None` and `iter.None2` are `iter.Seq`/`iter.Seq2`-compatible functions _themselves_, rather than returning functions of those types. The extra indirection of returning a function does not seem warranted here: a direct pointer to the package-level functions is sufficient. I intend them to read on the page as if they are named constants each representing a singleton empty sequence shared across the whole program.

## `Single`/`Single2`

These two utilities are essentially the single-value equivalents of `slices.Values` and `maps.Values` respectively, for situations where a specific concrete implementation of a sequence-returning interface only has one value to return, as opposed to concrete implementations in terms of slices or maps.

```go
type Example struct {
    singleThing string
}

func (e *Example) SeqReturningThing() iter.Seq[string] {
    return iter.Single(e.singleThing)
}
```

The goal is that a reader of this implementation can quickly recognize that it's returning exactly one value from the struct's state, without having to mentally parse the boilerplate and confirm that there's no special transformations hiding inside the `for` loop.

## `Map`/`Map2`

I expect that these are probably more controversial than the others because this moves from the world of sequence _construction_ into the world of sequence _transformation_, and thus potentially invites later proposals for other transformations such as filtering, reducing, flattening, etc.

However, I think _map in particular_ can be justified by the fact that sequences are not covariant and so I've encountered several situations where I'm writing out a boilerplate `for` loop purely to perform a subtyping coersion on every item in an iterator because my concrete implementation uses a concrete type but the interface I'm implementing uses an interface type, or because my concrete implementation uses a plain primitive type but the interface I'm implementing uses a named type with the same underlying type.

```go
type Messager {
    Message() string
}

type Concrete []string

func (c Concrete) SeqReturningThing() iter.Seq[Messager] {
    return iter.Map(slices.Values(c), func (s string) Messager {
        return stringMessager(s)
    })
}

type stringMessager string

func (sm stringMessager) Message() string {
    return string(sm)
}
```

I will immediately concede that I would probably also use `Map`/`Map2` for some non-coersion-related situations involving other trivial projections of items in the source sequence, such as extracting the value from a specific field of a struct type to return:

```go
type WithMessage struct {
    message string
    updated time.Time
}

type Example struct {
    withMessages []WithMessage
}

func (e Example) SeqReturningThing() iter.Seq[string] {
    return iter.Map(slices.Values(wm.withMessages, func (wm WithMessage) string {
        return wm.message
    })
}
```

Perhaps the temptation to use this for even more complicated situations would be too great, despite my limited intended use-cases of these functions. 🤷‍♂️ 

However, I think the crucial detail here is that `iter.Map` is not hiding any control flow beyond the boilerplate that's required for _any_ work with  `iter.Seq` values. This intentionally does not permit any conditional filtering, flattening, reducing, etc: for anything like that, I would expect an author to write a normal `for` loop over the sequence with `if` statements inside it, rather than trying to compose together various different sequence-combinator primitives.

## Potential Future Considerations

### Variadic generics, tuples, etc

As discussed in https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61898#issuecomment-2112841326, if a future version of the language were to support variadic generics, tuples, or any other means of concisely packing multiple values together into a single value then the `2`-suffixed variants of these operations would likely become redundant.

I don't think this is a significant concern for the reasons that were argued in that comment on the earlier proposal.

### Short function literal syntax

There have been a number of proposals for a shorter syntax for inline function literals where either the argument types, result types, or both are inferred from surrounding context rather than written explicitly.

If such a thing were introduced then it would become possible to use it with the `Map` and `Map2` functions. Here's an example using just one of the many possible short function literal syntaxes. (I'm not meaning to suggest that this particular choice is superior to any other. Use your imagination if you prefer a different style!)

```go
func (e Example) SeqReturningThing() iter.Seq[string] {
    return iter.Map(slices.Values(wm.withMessages), (wm) => wm.message)
}
```

This assumes that the spec for this feature would allow the compiler to infer that the `wm` argument is of type `WithMessage` (from one of my earlier examples) based on the type parameter of the `iter.Seq[V]` in the first argument, and that the return type of the closure is `string` based on the type of the `message` field in that struct type.

I don't really have any problem with the _current_ closure expression syntax for the relatively-simple ways I've proposed to use `Map`/`Map2` in this proposal, but I'm mentioning it just because this seems to have been a popular topic of discussion in earlier proposals.

### Other sequence combinators

I don't personally think the `iter` package should grow to include utilities for more complex transformations such as filtering, reducing, and flattening because that would move from the goal of just removing some uninteresting boilerplate into encouraging the hiding of control flow behind complex nested function calls.

For example, a hypothetical "reduce" function would often be better written as a plain `for` loop over the input sequence that just manipulates some local variables directly, without using any closures:

```go
    sum := 0
    for v := range seq {
        sum += v
    }
    return sum
```

Many of the other potential iterator-transforming operations _do_ involve at least one closure to represent the resulting sequence, but the body of that sequence function can just be plain code. For example, for filtering:

```go
    return func(yield func(string) bool) {
        for v := range s {
            if v == "" {
                continue
            }
            if !yield(v) {
                return
            }
        }
    }
```

The `if v == ""` statement is easy to read and understand. Expressing that condition instead as a `func (string) bool` is unnecessary indirection and I would argue that it _adds_ boilerplate rather than reducing it, while also obscuring what's going on.

My observations here apply mainly to the kind of combinator that takes at least one function pointer as an argument and calls it repeatedly. I don't feel nearly as strongly about functions that work on sequences _alone_, such as a hypothetical "zip" function that takes two `iter.Seq` of the same length and returns an `iter.Seq2` over both of them at once. I haven't personally encountered any need for such a function so far, but that particular utility is pretty annoying to hand-write inline.

### What about `Seq` to `Seq2` and `Seq2` to `Seq` transformations?

I have written such things a few times, but no single form of this has come up often enough for me to want to include it in this proposal.

The variations I've seen so far have been:

- Transform `Seq2` to `Seq` by discarding the first value of the pair.
- Transform `Seq2` to `Seq` by discarding the second value of the pair.
- Transform `Seq2` to `Seq`, or vice-versa, using an arbitrary callback function similar to `Map`/`Map2` in this proposal.

This implies at least four different possible functions that are all potentially defensible, but I've not included any of them in this proposal because I don't have sufficient experience needing them to argue for their inclusion in the standard library.

If they _were_ added at some later point after this proposal were accepted, the function-based ones would presumably have a design similar to `Map`/`Map2`, and the discarding ones would presumably have a design similar to `maps.Keys` and `maps.Values`. There would be no need to nest calls to these various "map"-like functions inside one another because they'd then cover every possible combination of single-value and two-value sequences so any reasonable projection could be written as call to just one of the functions.

### Argument order for `Map`/`Map2`

There was some specific contention in the earlier issue about whether function-based transformation functions should take the sequence first or the function first.

From what I read in the discussion, the argument in favor of function first is that it matches the design of the "map" functions in some other languages, such as Perl, and that it would read better if multiple such transformation functions were nested inside one another. On the other hand, the argument for sequence first is that the function argument is likely to be a multi-line closure expression in which case any arguments appearing after it would likely be missed by someone visually scanning over some source code, whereas sequence-first is more reminiscent of the ordering in a `for ... range` statement and so potentially easier to scan.

I've explicitly argued against encouraging such nesting of combinators, but I expect others will disagree with my arguments against that and so let's sketch what it might look like in both orders if we had both `Map` and `Filter` functions in future:

```go
// Sequence first, as in this proposal
return iter.Map(
    iter.Filter(
        seq,
        func (s string) bool {
            return s != ""
        }
    ),
    func (s string) string {
        return strings.ToUpper(s)
    },
)

// Function first, like in the earlier proposal
return iter.Map(
    func (s string) string {
        return strings.ToUpper(s)
    },
    iter.Filter(
        func (s string) bool {
            return s != ""
        },
        seq,
    ),
)
```

To be honest, I don't particularly like reading _either_ of these examples: regardless of order, this just seems hard to follow and understand what order everything would happen in. I _very marginally_ prefer the first because the filtering callback function appears before the mapping callback function on the page, which matches the order in which they'd presumably be called. But I expect others would have exactly the opposite preference, because that's just how these things tend to go. 🤷‍♂️ 

If this proposal were accepted and then someone later proposed a second function-based sequence transformation function then I expect we'd want them both to take their arguments in the same order, so unless we're willing to completely rule out there being any other functions added later I think it's a good idea to consider how they _could_ nest, even though this proposal intentionally avoids proposing anything that would be worth nesting in this way.

### Proposal Details

## Summary

This proposal requests the addition of a `Writer` type that implements `io.Writer` with built-in rate limiting capabilities to control write operations' bandwidth consumption. This feature would enable developers to easily implement bandwidth throttling for network connections, file operations, and other I/O scenarios.

## Motivation

### Current Problem

Currently, implementing rate limiting for I/O operations requires:
1. Manual integration of rate limiting logic with I/O operations
2. Scattered rate limiting code throughout applications
3. Complex coordination between multiple rate limiters (global vs per-connection)
4. No standardized approach for bandwidth control

### Use Cases

1. **Network Bandwidth Management**: Limit upload/download speeds for individual connections or globally
2. **Server Resource Protection**: Prevent individual clients from consuming excessive bandwidth
3. **Fair Resource Allocation**: Ensure equitable bandwidth distribution among multiple clients
4. **Background Process Throttling**: Limit bandwidth usage of background sync operations
5. **Testing and Simulation**: Simulate network conditions with controlled bandwidth

## Proposed Solution

### API Design

```go
// Writer is an io.Writer that applies rate limiting to write operations.
//
// For example, to limit a reader's read speed to 1KB/s:
//
//	limits := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(time.Second/1024.0), 1024)
//	reader := io.TeeReader(os.Stdin, NewWriter(limits))
//
// Or, to limit a writer's write speed to 1KB/s:
//
//	writer := io.MultiWriter(os.Stdout, NewWriter(limits))
type Writer struct {
	li *rate.Limiter
}

// Write writes data to the underlying writer, applying rate limiting based on
// the configured limits.
func (l *Writer) Write(p []byte) (int, error) {
	l.li.WaitN(context.Background(), len(p))
	return len(p), nil
}

// NewWriter creates a new Writer that limits write operations by a limiter.
func NewWriter(limiter *rate.Limiter) *Writer {
	return &Writer{li: limits}
}
```

### Usage Examples

#### Basic Reader Rate Limiting

```go
limits := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(time.Second/1024.0), 1024) // 1KB per second
reader := io.TeeReader(os.Stdin, NewWriter(limits))
reader.Read(...)
```

#### Basic Writer Rate Limiting

```go
limits := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(time.Second/1024.0), 1024) // 1KB per second
writer := io.MultiWriter(os.Stdout, NewWriter(limits))
writer.Write(...)
```

#### Network Connection Rate Limiting

Containing both global and per-connection limits:

```go
l, _ := net.Listen("tcp", "127.0.0.1:8080")

// A global connection limit for all connections. 1MB per second.
globalLimits := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(time.Second/1024/1024), 1024*1024)

for {
    cli, _ := l.Accept()

    // A single connection limit. 1KB per second.
    singleLimits := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(time.Second/1024), 1024) // 1KB per second

    // Make a reader that applies both global and single connection limits.
    var cliReader io.Reader = cli
    cliReader = io.TeeReader(cli, NewWriter(globalLimits))       // Apply global connection read limit
    cliReader = io.TeeReader(cliReader, NewWriter(singleLimits)) // Apply single connection read limit

    go func() {
        io.Copy(io.Discard, cliReader) // Discard all data read from the connection, simulating processing.
    }()
}
```

## Conclusion

The addition of a rate-limited `Writer` type would provide a clean, composable solution for bandwidth control in Go applications. It follows established patterns in the standard library (io.TeeReader and io.MultiWriter) while addressing a common need in network programming, file operations, and resource management scenarios.

This feature would significantly simplify the implementation of bandwidth-aware applications and provide a standardized approach to rate limiting across the Go ecosystem.

