### Proposal Details

Add `time.MustParse`, a panicking variant of `time.Parse`, following [`regexp.MustCompile`](https://pkg.go.dev/regexp#MustCompile), [`netip.MustParseAddr`](https://pkg.go.dev/net/netip#MustParseAddr) (Go 1.18), `uuid.MustParse` (Go 1.27, #62026), and the recently accepted `url.MustParse` (#79946):

```go
// MustParse is like [Parse] but panics if the value cannot be parsed.
// It is intended for use with hard-coded strings known to be valid,
// such as in tests and package-level variables.
func MustParse(layout, value string) Time
```

Codebases commonly define this helper themselves: GitHub code search currently returns [over 3,000 files](https://github.com/search?q=%22func+mustParseTime%28%22+language%3AGo&type=code) defining `func mustParseTime(` (the search is case-insensitive, so this includes the exported spelling). The Go repository itself defines it in [`src/encoding/json/v2/arshal_test.go`](https://github.com/golang/go/blob/master/src/encoding/json/v2/arshal_test.go):

```go
func mustParseTime(layout, value string) time.Time {
	t, err := time.Parse(layout, value)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	return t
}
```

The exported implementation would be identical.

`time.MustParse` was proposed before in #13328 and #15950 (2015/2016) but was not accepted at the time. Since then, package-specific Must parsing functions have become the established pattern: most recently, `url.MustParse` was split out of the general `must.Do` proposal (#54297, declined) and accepted on its own (#79946).

A common use case is tests, where keeping the expected value in the same textual representation as the fixture data can be clearer than translating it into a `time.Date` call:

```go
want := time.Date(2023, time.March, 14, 15, 9, 26, 787_000_000, time.UTC)

// vs.

want := time.MustParse(time.RFC3339Nano, "2023-03-14T15:09:26.787Z")
```

